  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "The question",
      "title": "Will one server do?",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "You’re designing PhotoShare. Before choosing any technology you need a sense of scale: is this one server, or a hundred?\n\nYou don’t need exact numbers — just the right **order of magnitude**. Napkin math gets you there in minutes.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Interviewers and architects both start here: rough numbers first, then design to fit them."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "?",
        "peak": "?",
        "servers": "?",
        "storage": "?"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Requests / s",
      "title": "Daily users → requests per second",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Start from what you know: users and how often they act. 10M users × 20 requests = 200M requests a day.\n\nA day has 86,400 seconds. Round it to **100,000** — napkin math is about being quick, not precise.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Handy shortcut: 1M requests per day ≈ 12 requests per second."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Average load ≈ 2,300 req/s"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "2,300",
        "peak": "?",
        "servers": "?",
        "storage": "?"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Peak",
      "title": "Plan for the peak, not the average",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Traffic isn’t flat: people browse in the evening and around big events. A typical peak is 2–3× the daily average.\n\nSize for 3× and the lone server is clearly overwhelmed.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "A system sized for its average load is down at exactly the moment most people use it."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Peak ≈ 3 × average ≈ 7,000 req/s",
        "~ One server: overloaded"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "2,300",
        "peak": "7,000",
        "servers": "?",
        "storage": "?"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Servers",
      "title": "How many servers?",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "A load test shows one server handles about **1,000 req/s**. Peak is 7,000, so we need 7 — plus headroom for spikes and for a server failing.\n\nCall it **10**. Notice we used a measured number, not a guess.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Spreading traffic across these servers is the job of a load balancer — see Module 02."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Web tier: 10 servers",
        "~ Each runs ~70% busy at peak"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "2,300",
        "peak": "7,000",
        "servers": "10",
        "storage": "?"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Storage",
      "title": "How much storage?",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Photos are the big one: 1M uploads × 2 MB = **2 TB per day**. Over a year that’s ~730 TB.\n\nData is usually stored three times so a dead disk loses nothing — so plan for about **2 PB** in year one. That rules out “one database server” immediately.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Storage grows every day and never resets. Requests per second come and go; bytes stay."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Storage tier",
        "+ 2 TB/day → ~730 TB/year",
        "~ ×3 copies → ~2 PB"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "2,300",
        "peak": "7,000",
        "servers": "10",
        "storage": "730 TB"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Bandwidth",
      "title": "How much network?",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Uploads are the small part: 2 TB a day is about 20 MB/s coming in. Viewing is the big part: if each user looks at 100 photos a day as 200 KB thumbnails, that’s **200 TB a day** going out.\n\n200 TB ÷ 100,000 s ≈ 2 GB/s — about 16 Gbit/s on average, more at peak. That’s a job for a CDN, not ten web servers.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Read-heavy media apps are usually limited by bandwidth before CPU. Estimate bytes out, not just requests."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ CDN serves the images",
        "+ Egress ≈ 16 Gbit/s average"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "2,300",
        "peak": "7,000",
        "servers": "10",
        "storage": "730 TB"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Cache size",
      "title": "Will it fit in memory?",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Feeds read photo metadata and user profiles constantly. Could a cache hold the hot part? Profiles: 10M × 2 KB = 20 GB. Today’s new photos: 1M × 1 KB = 1 GB.\n\nAbout 21 GB — it fits in the memory of a single cache server (with a second for redundancy). No exotic architecture needed.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Napkin math often says “this is smaller than you think”. That’s just as valuable as finding something big."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Cache: ~21 GB hot data"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "2,300",
        "peak": "7,000",
        "servers": "10",
        "storage": "730 TB"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Numbers",
      "title": "Numbers worth memorising",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "A handful of numbers make most estimates possible. Learn the orders of magnitude, not the decimals.\n\n• Read from memory — 100 ns\n• Read from SSD — 100 µs\n• Round trip, same data center — 0.5 ms\n• Round trip, across an ocean — 75–150 ms\n• Seconds in a day — ~100,000",
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "2,300",
        "peak": "7,000",
        "servers": "10",
        "storage": "730 TB"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "sc": {
          "effect": "none"
        },
        "cu": {
          "effect": "none"
        },
        "wc": {
          "effect": "none"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Recap",
      "title": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "body": "• **Users × actions ÷ 100k s** gives average requests per second.\n• Multiply by **2–3×** for the peak, and size for that.\n• **Servers** = peak ÷ per-server capacity, plus headroom.\n• **Storage** = items/day × size × 365 × copies.\n• Estimate **bytes out** and **memory** too — they often decide the design.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "That finishes Foundations. Next: **Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling** — what to do when the numbers say one machine won’t do."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "dau": "10M",
        "avg": "2,300",
        "peak": "7,000",
        "servers": "10",
        "storage": "730 TB"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "sc": {
          "effect": "none"
        },
        "cu": {
          "effect": "none"
        },
        "wc": {
          "effect": "none"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
